\layout Itemize

The `death rate' 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset 

 has different definitions in different parts of the state space.
 
\layout Itemize

We need to consider detailed balance for each part in turn.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Detailed Balance
\layout Itemize

The Erlang delay system may be analysed as a birth-death process with birth
 rate 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda_{k}=\lambda\]

\end_inset 

 and death rate 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mu_{k}=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
k\mu & k=0,1,\dots,m-1\\
m\mu & k>m\end{array}\right.\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

The Two Situations
\layout Itemize

This means we have to consider two situations in detailed balance
\begin_deeper 
\layout Enumerate

When the system's state, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

, is less than or equal to the number of servers.
\layout Enumerate

When the system's state, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

, is greater than the number of servers.
\layout Standard

We will cover this as an example.
\end_deeper 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Stationary Distribution
\layout Itemize

The overall stationary distribution is then
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
P\left(0\right)\frac{(m\rho)^{k}}{k!}, & k\leq m\\
P\left(0\right)\frac{m^{m}\rho^{k}}{m!} & k>m\end{array}\right.\]

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\left[\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}\frac{\left(m\rho\right)^{k}}{k!}+\frac{\left(m\rho\right)^{m}}{m!\left(1-\rho\right)}\right]^{-1}.\]

\end_inset 

Note that this relies on 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho<1$
\end_inset 

, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\geq m\mu$
\end_inset 

 the system has no stationary distribution.
\end_deeper 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Probability of Waiting
\layout Itemize

For the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m$
\end_inset 

 queue a key quantity of interest is the probability of of a customer queueing,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{Q}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

What is the probability, when the system is at steady state, that a customer
 will arrive and find all the 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 servers busy? 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k\geq m\right) & = & \sum_{k=m}^{\infty}P\left(k\right)\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\geq m\right)=P\left(0\right)\sum_{k=m}^{\infty}\frac{m^{m}\rho^{k}}{m!}\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\geq m\right)=P\left(0\right)\frac{\left(\rho m\right)^{m}}{m!}\sum_{k=m}^{\infty}\rho^{k-m},\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Probability of Waiting contd
\layout Itemize

Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{k}=k-m$
\end_inset 

 in the sum gives
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\geq m\right)=P\left(0\right)\frac{\left(\rho m\right)^{m}}{m!}\sum_{\dot{k}=1}^{\infty}\rho^{\dot{k}},\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Making the usual substitution this can be re-written
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
C\left(m,a\right)=P\left(k\geq m\right)=P\left(0\right)\frac{\left(\rho m\right)^{m}}{m!\left(1-\rho\right)}.\label{eq:ErlangCone}\end{equation}

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Erlang C Formula
\layout Itemize

This is known as the 
\emph on 
Erlang C formula
\emph default 
, or Erlang's second formula after A.K.
 Erlang.
 By making use of our definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset 

 we can rewrite the offered load as
\begin_inset Formula \[
a=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}=m\rho\]

\end_inset 

 and substituting into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:ErlangCone}

\end_inset 

) we have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
C(m,a)=\frac{P\left(0\right)a^{m}}{m!\left(1-\frac{a}{m}\right)}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Computing the Erlang C Formula
\layout Itemize

Can be computed on a computer: but 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

some care must be taken when computing it.
 
\layout Itemize

when 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 is large overflow problems will occur for 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{m}$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m!$
\end_inset 

.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize

Compute the formula in log space,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\ln C\left(m,a\right)=\ln P\left(0\right)+m\ln a-\ln m!-\ln\left(1-\frac{a}{m}\right).\]

\end_inset 

Still problems with 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln P\left(0\right)$
\end_inset 

 term, but each of the other terms can be computed with appropriate functions
 in MATLAB.
 
\layout Itemize

When we need to compute the Erlang C curve, for the purposes of exams and
 exercises, we will use the old fashioned method of curves.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Erlang C Curves
\layout Itemize

We have provided you with two sets of curves in your notes.
 
\layout Itemize

In an exam, if they are need curves will be provided with the exam paper.
 
\layout Itemize

Make sure you mark the curves and hand them in with your paper.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Examples
\layout Itemize

Examples 2 and 3 at the end of the chapter cover the Erlang C curves.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Average number in System.
\layout Itemize

We can also compute the average number of calls in the Erlang Delay system,
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\frac{\rho}{1-\rho}C(m,a)+m\rho\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

These two terms represent the average queue length and the average number
 of busy lines.
 
\layout Itemize

The average queue length is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{Q}=\frac{\rho}{1-\rho}C(m,a)\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Average Queueing Time
\layout Itemize

Using Little's formula the average waiting time is simply 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\bar{N}_{Q}}{\lambda}=\frac{C(m,a)}{m\mu(1-\rho)}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Note that this is the average queueing time for all customers (whether they
 queued or not).
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Queueing Time for those who Queue
\layout Itemize

Use the fact that the average queueing time for those who don't queue is
 zero.
\layout Itemize

The average queueing time can be decomposed as
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=C(m,a)\bar{W}_{Q}+\left(1-C(m,a)\right)\bar{W}_{\sim Q}\]

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize


\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}_{Q}$
\end_inset 

 is the average queueing time for those who do queue 
\layout Itemize


\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}_{\sim Q}$
\end_inset 

 is the average queueing time for those who don't queue.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}_{\sim Q}=0$
\end_inset 

 the second term in this sum is zero we can rewrite in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}_{Q}$
\end_inset 

 as 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W_{Q}}=\frac{\bar{W}}{C(m,a)}=\frac{1}{m\mu(1-\rho)}=\frac{1}{\mu(m-a)}\]

\end_inset 

so the average queueing time for those who do queue is independent of the
 probability of queueing.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

