\layout Itemize

From the figure the wait for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

th customer will be given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
W_{i}=R_{i}+\sum_{j=i-(k_{i}-1)}^{i-1}X_{j}\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

Summing over both sides (
\emph on 
i.e.
 
\emph default 
for all the arriving customers up to the 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset 

th) 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{i=1}^{I}W_{i}=\sum_{i=1}^{I}R_{i}+\sum_{i=1}^{I}\sum_{j=i-(k_{i}-1)}^{i-1}X_{j}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Dividing through by 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset 

 gives us 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{i=1}^{I}\frac{W_{i}}{I}=\sum_{i=1}^{I}\frac{R_{i}}{I}+\sum_{i=1}^{I}\sum_{j=i-(k_{i}-1)}^{i-1}\frac{X_{j}}{I}\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

In the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $I\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 this becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lim_{I\rightarrow\infty}\sum_{i=1}^{I}\frac{W_{i}}{I}=\lim_{I\rightarrow\infty}\sum_{i=1}^{I}\frac{R_{i}}{I}+\lim_{I\rightarrow\infty}\sum_{i=1}^{I}\frac{(k_{i}-1)X_{i}}{I}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

The terms of which are recognised as 
\emph on 
time averages
\emph default 
.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Time Average Equivalence
\layout Itemize

If we assume 
\emph on 
time averages
\emph default 
 are equivalent to 
\emph on 
steady state averages
\emph default 
 this gives 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle W\right\rangle =\left\langle R\right\rangle +\left\langle \left(k-1\right)X\right\rangle .\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

The length of the queue as seen by the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

th arrival is independent of the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

th arrival's length (our second assumption)
\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

this means 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle (k-1)X\right\rangle =\left\langle k-1\right\rangle \left\langle X\right\rangle $
\end_inset 

 and since 
\begin_inset Formula $(k-1)$
\end_inset 

 is the length of the queue we may write 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\bar{R}+\bar{N}_{Q}\bar{X}\]

\end_inset 

 
\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize

Using Little's theorem for the queue we have
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{Q}=\lambda\bar{W}\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

Substituting back in we have
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\bar{R}+\lambda\bar{X}\bar{W}.\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Wait in Terms of Residual Service Time
\layout Itemize

From the previous slide:
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\bar{R}+\lambda\bar{X}\bar{W}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

And the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\lambda\bar{X}$
\end_inset 

 (which stems from Little's formula for the service) gives us 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\bar{R}+\rho\bar{W}\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

Leading to 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\bar{W}=\frac{\bar{R}}{1-\rho}\label{eqn:arrivals}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

We now have the average queueing time in terms of the mean residual service
 time.
 To complete the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula we simply need to calculate
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{R}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Mean Residual Service Time from Departures Perspective
\layout Itemize

We again turn to a graphical proof to obtain the value of the mean residual
 service time.
 
