Here we have assumed that there is no further delay associated with each
 link (propagation, processing).
 In practice such delays may arise however we shall not consider them.
 See Bertsekas & Gallager Section 3.6.1 for more details.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Jackson's Theorem
\layout Itemize

While Kleinrock's independence assumption approximates the activities at
 each queue as 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queues, Jackson's theorem provides a result for the number in the queueing
 system as a whole.
 
\layout Itemize

Some analysis of the system is given in Section\SpecialChar ~
3.8 of Bertsekas and Gallager.
 
\layout Itemize

For our purposes it is sufficient to give the assumptions and the result.
 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

there is no correlation between service times and queue lengths.
 
\layout Itemize

randomisation is used to divide the traffic along different routes through
 the network.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize

Given these assumptions the average number of packets in the network is
 given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
N=\sum_{i,j}\frac{\lambda_{ij}}{\mu_{ij}-\lambda_{ij}}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Network Simulation
\layout Itemize

The approximations we have outlined so far may be useful for `back of the
 envelope' style calculations.
 
\layout Itemize

In practice though it is highly likely that you will wish to simulate the
 system.
 
\layout Itemize

There are a range of simulation packages available.
 
\layout Itemize

We will introduce a simple one based on the Tcl scripting language called
 `The Network Simulator' or ns-2 (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{http://www.isi.edu/nsnam/ns/}

\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

It is a discrete event simulator aimed at networking research.
 
\layout Itemize

It supports a range of protocols including TCP and multicast protocols and
 it is straightforward to extend using C++.
 
\layout Itemize

It is a powerful research tool which has the added advantage of being freely
 available.
 
\layout Itemize

It is installed in the Lewin Laboratory on the Linux systems.
 
\layout Itemize

If you require a personal installation, it can be installed through the
 Cygwin (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{http://www.cygwin.com}

\end_inset 

) API.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Network Simulation with NS2
\layout Itemize

As a lab class in week 9 you will simulate some simple queues in NS2 and
 compare the simulations with some results obtained using Kleinrock's independen
ce assumption.
 
\layout Itemize

The lab class will be completed in Linux so please make sure you know your
 Unix password for the DCS system before the lab start.
 
\layout Itemize

The lab sheet has some notes on simple commands you will need.
 
\layout Itemize

If you are unfamiliar with Linux it is suggested that you try them out before
 starting the lab.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Multiaccess Systems
\layout Itemize

Finally we briefly review multiaccess systems.
 
\layout Itemize

These are systems where there is one communication channel and multiple
 stations (or nodes) making use of it.
 
\layout Itemize

Examples of multiaccess systems include: Satellite systems; packet radio
 networks; a multitapped bus.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

LANs
\layout Itemize

A very common example of multi-access channels is the local area network.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

In a local area network transmission rates are around 10Mbps to 1Gbps.
 
\layout Itemize

Transmission distances range from a few meters to several kilometres.
 
\layout Itemize

The required error rates are low: typically from 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{-8}$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{-11}$
\end_inset 

, that's about 1 error every Gigabyte.
\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize

Typically we are interested in the throughput of the network --- the amount
 of data or messages passing through the network each second.
 
\layout Itemize

If the number of overhead bits (associated with network administration 
\emph on 
etc
\emph default 
.) is low then this is approximately equal to the utilization factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset 

.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Channel Multiplexing
\layout Itemize

Until the introduction of the ALOHA network the standard approach to splitting
 a channel was through multiplexing.
 
\layout Itemize

We will now briefly review this approach to multiplexing using Example\SpecialChar ~
3.9
 from Bertsekas and Gallagher.
\layout Itemize

Assume we have 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 statistically independent Poisson streams.
 
\layout Itemize

Each has arrival rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\lambda}{m}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

The packets to be transmitted are assumed to have an exponentially distributed
 length.
\layout Itemize

For a given bandwidth this leads to a service time distribution for the
 whole transmission line which is an exponential with a rate parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Statistical Multiplexing
\layout Itemize

System 1: merge the Poisson streams together, through statistical multiplexing.
 
\layout Itemize

The superposition property of the Poisson distribution tells us that they
 become a Poisson stream with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

If the transmission times are exponentially distributed the average delay,
 from the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue, will be
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\frac{1}{\mu-\lambda}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Time or Frequency Division Multiplexing
\layout Itemize

System 2: provide each communication channel with a separate `circuit'.
\layout Itemize

The transmission line needs to be split into 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 separate channels.
 
\layout Itemize

Each of these separate channels will now have service time distributions
 with a reduced rated 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\mu}{m}$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize

Delay in this system will now be
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\frac{1}{\frac{\mu}{m}-\frac{\lambda}{m}}=\frac{m}{\mu-\lambda}\]

\end_inset 

which is 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 times larger than that for statistical multiplexing.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Why does this happen?
\layout Standard

The reduction in delay arises from the fact that when only one of the 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 channels is transmitting with statistical multiplexing it can use the entire
 bandwidth of the channel but with time or frequency division multiplexing
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{m-1}{m}$
\end_inset 

 of the bandwidth is wasted.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Multiaccess Systems
\layout Itemize

In multiaccess systems splitting the channel using time division multiplexing
 is known as time division multiple access (TDMA).
 
\layout Itemize

Similarly splitting the channel using frequency division multiplexing is
 known as frequency division multiple access (FDMA).
\layout Itemize

We have already shown that statistical multiplexing can make more efficient
 use of bandwidth than time or frequency based multiplexing.
 
\layout Itemize

However in a multiaccess system it can be difficult to implement in the
 manner discussed above.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Multiplexing for Multiaccess
\layout Itemize

We assumed that the all the transmissions could be queued in a single 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue.
 
\layout Itemize

In a local area network the transmitting stations (or nodes) are distributed
 across different portions of the network as are the receiving nodes.
\layout Itemize

Forming a single queue is not possible without some form of centralised
 control.
 
\layout Itemize

TDMA and FDMA can be implemented by by dedicating one channel for each station
 to transmit on, but allowing each station to receive on any channel.
 
\layout Itemize

This allows for an analysis of the type we described above.
 However, it is not clear how to perform statistical multiplexing in these
 circumstances.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Random Access
\layout Itemize

When we have stations distributed across a single communication channel
 would be to have a centralised computer attempting to control which computer
 is able to access our transmission lines at what times.
 
\layout Itemize

However, we may wish communication to occur without centralised control.
 In this case we may choose a 
\emph on 
random access
\emph default 
 solution.
\layout Itemize

We will consider random access networks where there is one communication
 channel on which all the nodes communicate
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed false

\layout Standard

It could be the case that only a subset of the nodes have access to the
 channel.
 
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

Each station sends a packet immediately whenever it has a packet to send.
 This means that packets can collide and may have to be resent.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Pure ALOHA Networks
\layout Itemize

ALOHA network was developed at the University of Hawaii.
\layout Itemize

Packet radio network which communicated between different stations on the
 Hawaiian islands.
 
\layout Itemize

For the ALOHA network 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

all stations attempt to communicate on the same channel.
 
\layout Itemize

When a station attempts to transmit, it detects if its transmission has
 interfered with an existing transmission: this is known as a 
\emph on 
collision
\emph default 
.
 
\layout Itemize

If a collision occurs the station becomes 
\emph on 
backlogged 
\emph default 
and attempts to transmit at a later time.
 
\layout Itemize

If both stations attempted to retransmit after the same period of time they
 would simply collide again.
 
\layout Itemize

To avoid this problem the stations normally wait for a random period of
 time before retransmitting.
\end_deeper 
\layout ShortFoilhead

ALOHA
\layout Itemize

For this network we assume that each station can tell whether the transmitted
 packets were correctly received.
 
\layout Itemize

Furthermore, we assume that each packet requires 1 `time unit' for transmission.
 This means that if a packet of interest is sent at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, then any other packet sent between time 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset 

 and time 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset 

 will collide with our packet.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/pureALOHA.eps
	width 100text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Pure ALOHA, packets collide with any other packet transmitted within one
 time unit of their transmission.
 The diagram shows a packet sent at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 and packets sent at 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset 

.
 Any packet sent after 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset 

 and before 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset 

 will collide with the packet sent at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

.
\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Simple Analysis
\layout Itemize

The arrival rate for packets at all stations is a Poisson process with rate
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda.$
\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

We assume that the number of stations is very large (infinite) so that an
 individual station's packet rate is an insignificant proportion of 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, 
\emph on 
i.e.

\emph default 
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{\lambda_{n}}{\lambda}=\lim_{\delta\rightarrow0}\delta=0.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

We denote the number of backlogged stations at any particular time as 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Backlogged Stations
\layout Itemize

A station is considered backlogged between the time that it became aware
 that its previous transmission failed and the time of a successful transmission.
\layout Itemize

Once a station is aware that a transmission was unsuccessful it waits for
 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i}$
\end_inset 

 time units to retransmit where the distribution over these waits is given
 by
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(T_{i}\right)=\mu\exp\left(-\mu T_{i}\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

If 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 nodes are backlogged then the arrivals from backlogged nodes is a Poisson
 process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $n\mu$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize

The overall `arrival process' for the system is Poisson with a rate 
\begin_inset Formula $G\left(n\right)=\lambda+n\mu$
\end_inset 

.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Pure ALOHA
\layout Itemize

The probability that there are no arrivals in an interval of 2 time units
 for a Poisson process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $G\left(n\right)$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(k=0|G\left(n\right),2\right)=\exp\left(-2G\left(n\right)\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Therefore this is the probability that there is no collision between the
 packet of interest and any other packets in the network.
\layout Itemize

It is therefore the probability of a successful transmission.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Throughput
\layout Itemize

The rate of attempted transmissions is 
\begin_inset Formula $G\left(n\right)$
\end_inset 

 so the rate of successful transmissions is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
G^{\prime}\left(n\right)=G\left(n\right)\exp\left(-2G\left(n\right)\right),\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

This is known as the throughput.
 
\layout Itemize

It is the number of packets that successfully reach their destination.
 The throughput is plotted as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset 

 .
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed true

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset ERT
status Open

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/pureALOHAThroughput.eps
	width 60text%

\end_inset 


\begin_inset ERT
status Open

\layout Standard
}{8}
\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Throughput for pure ALOHA as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset 

.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:pureALOHAThroughput}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Maximum Throughput
\layout Itemize

The curve goes up to a maximum point and drops down thereafter.
 
\layout Itemize

We can find the maximum of the throughput by looking for points where the
 gradient of the throughput is zero.
 
\layout Itemize

Taking the gradient of 
\begin_inset Formula $G^{\prime}$
\end_inset 

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset 

 gives
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{dG^{\prime}}{dG}=\exp\left(-2G\right)-2G\exp\left(-2G\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Stationary Points
\layout Itemize

To find the stationary points we set this equation to zero and solve for
 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset 

.
 The equation becomes
\begin_inset Formula \[
\exp\left(-2G\right)-2G\exp\left(-2G\right)=0\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
\left(1-2G\right)\exp\left(-2G\right)=0\]

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

So either 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp\left(-2G\right)=0$
\end_inset 

, which in turn implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $G\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 or 
\layout Itemize


\begin_inset Formula $1-2G=0$
\end_inset 

 which implies 
\begin_inset Formula $G=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

The first solution is a minimum (and the throughput is zero) and the second
 solution is a maximum.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize

The maximum is the maximum possible throughput for the system and is given
 when 
\begin_inset Formula $G=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Maximum Throughput
\layout Itemize

Substituting into the equation for the throughput we find that the maximum
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}\exp\left(-1\right)=\frac{1}{2e}\approx0.184$
\end_inset 

.
 This maximum throughput shows that at best the channel is in use only about
 18% of the time
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed false

\layout Standard

Note that since the service time is fixed at 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{n}=1$
\end_inset 

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 then the utilisation for this system is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\lambda\bar{X}=\lambda.$
\end_inset 


\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

The system will be in equilibrium if the throughput equals 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Analysis of Backlog Delay
\layout Itemize

Recall our assumption that a backlogged node waits for a time, 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i}$
\end_inset 

, before attempting to retransmit, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(T_{i}\right)=\mu\exp\left(-\mu T_{i}\right).\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Itemize

Given this assumption, can we compute the delay associated with this backlogged
 node? 
\layout Itemize

Since the node may experience several collisions, the wait associated with
 the backlog will involve a sum over the random variable 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i}$
\end_inset 

.
 It turns out to be an 
\emph on 
Erlang distribution
\emph default 
.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Sums of Exponential Variables and the Erlang Distribution
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:erlang}

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

If a random variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

, is exponentially distributed with rate parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset 

.
 The sum of 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset 

 random variables, each of which is exponentially distributed with rate
 parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset 

 is distributed as an 
\emph on 
Erlang distribution.
 
\layout Itemize

Consider variables a set of 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset 

 variables
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x_{1}\dots x_{a}\right)$
\end_inset 

 ,
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(x_{i}|b\right)=b\exp\left(-bx_{i}\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

We define the sum of the values to be, 
\begin_inset Formula $z=\sum_{i=1}^{a}x_{i}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

The distribution governing 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset 

 is then an 
\emph on 
Erlang distribution
\emph default 
, given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(z|a,b\right)=\frac{b^{a}}{\left(a-1\right)!}z^{a-1}\exp\left(-bz\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Notes on Erlang
\layout Itemize

Note that this distribution is a generalisation of the exponential distribution
 because the exponential is the special case where 
\begin_inset Formula $a=1$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

This clearly should be the case because if 
\begin_inset Formula $a=1$
\end_inset 

 then the `sum' of that variable will simply be the variable itself.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Erlang Distribution Mean and Variance
\layout Standard

The mean of the Erlang distribution is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle z\right\rangle _{p\left(z|a,b\right)}=\frac{a}{b}\]

\end_inset 

 and the variance is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\textrm{var}\left(z\right)=\frac{a}{b^{2}}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Delay Associated with Backlogs
\layout Itemize

If the node retransmits 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 times then the distribution over the total time required, 
\begin_inset Formula $W=\sum_{i=1}^{k}T_{i}$
\end_inset 

, comes from the sum of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 independent exponentially distributed variables.
 
\layout Itemize

We know that the resulting variable 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset 

 is Erlang distributed with parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(W|k,\mu\right)=\frac{\mu^{k}}{\left(a-1\right)!}W^{k-1}\exp\left(-\mu W\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

The probability of transmitting after 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 attempts is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(k\right)=q\left(1-q\right)^{k-1}.\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset 

 is the probability of a successful transmission
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed true

\layout Standard

Compare this with the ARQ system we analysed in the chapter on 
\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset 

 queues.
 There we were considering errors (unsuccessful transmissions) so 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\left(1-q\right)$
\end_inset 

.
\end_inset 

.
 For pure ALOHA 
\begin_inset Formula $q=\exp\left(-2G\right)$
\end_inset 

.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Distribution for 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

From the product rule and the sum rule we know that the probability distribution
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset 

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
p\left(W|\mu\right) & = & \sum_{k=1}^{\infty}p\left(W|k,\mu\right)p\left(k\right),\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(W|\mu\right)=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}q\left(1-q\right)^{k-1}\frac{\mu^{k}}{\left(k-1\right)!}W^{k-1}\exp\left(-\mu W\right),\]

\end_inset 

substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{k}=k-1$
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(W|\mu\right)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}q\left(1-q\right)^{\dot{k}}\frac{\mu^{\dot{k}+1}}{\dot{k}!}X^{\dot{k}}\exp\left(-\mu W\right),\]

\end_inset 

extracting the 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset 

, the exponential term and one of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

s from the product we rewrite as,
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
p\left(X|\mu\right)=q\mu\exp\left(-\mu W\right)\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{\left(\mu\left(1-q\right)W\right)^{\dot{k}}}{\dot{k}!},\label{eq:pXgivenMuWithSum}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

The term inside the sum is then recognised as being of the form
\begin_inset Formula \[
\exp\left(x\right)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^{\dot{k}}}{\dot{k}!}\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{\left(\mu\left(1-q\right)W\right)^{\dot{k}}}{\dot{k}!}=\exp\left(\mu\left(1-q\right)W\right),\]

\end_inset 

which can be substituted back into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:pXgivenMuWithSum}

\end_inset 

) to obtain
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
p\left(W|\mu\right) & = & q\mu\exp\left(-\mu W\right)\exp\left(\mu\left(1-q\right)W\right),\\
 & = & q\mu\exp\left(-q\mu W\right)\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

which is recognised as an exponential distribution with rate parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu^{\prime}=q\mu=\mu\exp\left(-2G\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

Using the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset 

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

 we can write
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mu^{\prime}=\mu\exp\left(-2\left(\lambda+n\mu\right)\right).\label{eq:rateFunctionALOHA}\end{equation}

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

So the service rate is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Optimum value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

For a given value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 there is a optimal value for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

This value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

 can be found by seeking the maximum rate, this is done by differentiating
 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{d\mu^{\prime}}{d\mu}=\exp\left(-2\left(\lambda+n\mu\right)\right)-2n\mu\exp\left(-2\left(\lambda+n\mu\right)\right)\]

\end_inset 

and setting the result to zero to find the stationary points,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\exp\left(-2\left(\lambda+n\mu\right)\right)-2n\mu\exp\left(-2\left(\lambda+n\mu\right)\right)=0,\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
\left(1-2n\mu\right)\exp\left(-2\left(\lambda+n\mu\right)\right)=0.\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Solutions for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

There are two solutions to this equation.
 The first is 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp\left(-2\left(\lambda+n\mu\right)\right)=0$
\end_inset 

, meaning that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda+n\mu\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

.
 This solution is a minimiser .
 The other solution is given when 
\begin_inset Formula \[
0=1-2n\mu\]

\end_inset 

or
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mu=\frac{1}{2n},\]

\end_inset 

 this turns out to be a maximum of (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:rateFunctionALOHA}

\end_inset 

).
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Notes on Optimum
\layout Itemize

By maximising the `rate' parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu^{\prime}$
\end_inset 

 we minimise the average delay associated with the backlog, which is given
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}=\frac{1}{\mu^{\prime}}$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize

By setting 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=\frac{1}{2n}$
\end_inset 

 we find a minimum for the average delay: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}_{\textrm{min}}=2n\exp\left(2\lambda+1\right)\]

\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

In practice it may be difficult to set the rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

 to something that is dependent on the number of backlogged nodes.
 
\layout Itemize

This is because the number of backlogged nodes can only be known by nodes
 communicating their status over the network, we have not allowed for such
 a mechanism in our analysis.
 
\layout Itemize

Note that the minimum average delay goes up linearly with the number of
 backlogged nodes and 
\emph on 
exponentially
\emph default 
 with the rate of arrivals for the system, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Slotted ALOHA
\layout Itemize

A simple improvement to the ALOHA system comes from constraining when packets
 can be sent.
 
\layout Itemize

Instead of allowing stations to transmit immediately they receive a packet
 each station waits for the beginning of a `slot' to start transmitting.
 
\layout Itemize

These slots are synchronised across the entire network.
 
\layout Itemize

In this system a collision occurs if another station attempts to transmit
 in the same slot.
 
\layout Itemize

This happens if another station received a packet for transmission in the
 preceding slot.
\layout Itemize

If each slot is one time unit in length the probability of this event is
 given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(k=0|G\left(n\right),2\right)=\exp\left(-G\left(n\right)\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout ShortFoilhead

Slotted ALOHA
\layout Itemize

The probability is smaller than for pure ALOHA because the `vulnerable period'
 for a collision is smaller (half the length of before).
 
\layout Itemize

A collision now only occurs if another station attempts to transmit in the
 same slot.
 As a result the rate of successful transmissions is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
G^{\prime}\left(n\right)=G\left(n\right)\exp\left(-G\left(n\right)\right),\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard


